{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>And Zipporah took a flint.</b> Tsur means a sharp thing, like \"flints of Tsor\" (Joshua 5:2), which are sharp, and likewise \"and you shall turn back the Tsor of his sword\" (Psalms 89:44)." "And the sense of \"and she touched his feet\" — in my opinion, to the feet of Moses, in the manner of \"and He will see the blood on the lintel… and He will not let the destroyer enter\" (below, 12:23). Therefore \"and He let him go\" — from him, from Moses — for the illness and trembling ceased. And Rabbi Samuel said that \"his feet\" means the feet of Eliezer as well, and \"He let him go\" accordingly. If so, one may object to him: if the illness was upon Eliezer, how did she circumcise him to add pain upon his pain?" "And it is the custom of women to call a son, when he is circumcised, a bridegroom." "And the sense of \"damim\": \"for a man of blood you are to me\" (2 Samuel 16:8), for on your account my husband will die."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:31:44.681976193Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}